==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*gʷem-}}
From {{etyl|frm|en}} {{m|frm|contravenir}} (French {{m|fr|contrevenir}}), from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|contraveniō}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˌkɒn.tɹəˈviːn/}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[act]] [[contrary]] to an [[order]]; to [[fail]] to [[conform]] to a [[regulation]] or [[obligation]].
#: {{syn|en|breach|break|infringe|violate}}
#* '''1648''', {{w|Samuel Rutherford}}, ''A Survey of the Spirituall Antichrist,'' London: Andrew Crooke, Chapter{{nbsp}}69, p.{{nbsp}}141,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A57980.0001.001]</sup>
#*: {{...}} nothing is a commandement, or a commanded dutie but that which if we '''contravene''', it maketh us guilty of sin before God,
#* '''1713''', {{w|Daniel Defoe}}, ''Considerations upon the eighth and ninth articles of the treaty of commerce and navigation,'' London: J. Baker, p.{{nbsp}}8,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004834062.0001.000]</sup>
