#* {{RQ:Douglass Bondage|section=Appendix|page=436|passage=I have shown that slavery is wicked {{...}} in that it '''contravenes''' the laws of eternal justice, and tramples in the dust all the humane and heavenly precepts of the New Testament.}}
#* '''1872''', {{w|George Eliot}}, ''{{w|Middlemarch}},'' London: William Blackwood, Volume{{nbsp}}3, Book{{nbsp}}5, Chapter{{nbsp}}45, p.{{nbsp}}44,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/middlemarchstudy31elio/page/44]</sup>
#*: {{...}} the other medical visitors having a consultative influence, but no power to '''contravene''' Lydgate’s ultimate decisions;
#* '''1919''', {{w|Henry Blake Fuller}}, ''{{w|Bertram Cope's Year|Bertram Cope’s Year}},'' Chicago: Ralph Fletcher Seymour, Chapter{{nbsp}}2, p.{{nbsp}}19,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/bertramcopesyear00full/page/18]</sup>
